export const body = "<p>Game design is a prevalent yet nebulous concept. Game makers (artists and non-artists) hear this term up almost every day at work, but rarely do they have a firm grasp on its meaning. In this article, we'll demystify what game design is and what game designers do, so everyone has a shared vocabulary to discuss the topic in-depth.</p>\n\n<p>For the uninitiated, game designers create the gameplay: what is doable and what isn't. Let’s take <em>Super Mario Bros.</em> as an example. First, the designer defines what Mario is capable of: walking, jumping, and sprinting. Then, he defines the interactions between Mario and his enemies: Mario defeats his enemy by stomping on them, but will collapse if they touch him. Finally, the designer populates the levels with pick-up items and obstacles to make them fun to play.</p>\n\n<p>Now that we understand the basics, let's dig deeper.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/fa10d2c6fe016ebb2e71.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/fa10d2c6fe016ebb2e71.webp\" width=\"1075\" height=\"580\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/593002e1d145f7f7fc71.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/593002e1d145f7f7fc71.webp\" width=\"1031\" height=\"580\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/28a85ab883aaa12b7338.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/28a85ab883aaa12b7338.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"738\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Common types of game designers</h2>\n\n<p>Since <em>Mario</em>, games have become increasingly complex. Because of that, game design is no longer a singular discipline, but branches out to several paths. However, the three broadest and commonest categories of game design are:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Content design</strong></li><li><strong>System design</strong></li><li><strong>Combat design</strong></li><li><strong>Level design</strong></li></ul>\n\n<p>Each category above contains various sub-disciplines, which we will brush over in their respective sections.</p>\n\n<h2>Content design</h2>\n\n<p>Content designers (also known as game writers) are responsible for the <strong>story-related content</strong>, including the backstory for the game world and its characters. They also write the game script to define the narrative and dialogue. Their impact is most visible when you engage with the story.</p>\n\n<p>Aside from the story, content designers also handle other text-based content such as item descriptions and bestiary writings.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/8541bcf6d666f330c40b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/8541bcf6d666f330c40b.webp\" width=\"1030\" height=\"580\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/ab9914f9ab6f3fdb84a7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/ab9914f9ab6f3fdb84a7.webp\" width=\"1039\" height=\"584\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/d481dc7ddd0e4b30bb70.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/d481dc7ddd0e4b30bb70.webp\" width=\"733\" height=\"580\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>System design</h2>\n\n<p>System designers build game mechanics and handle the balancing. Basically, they define the ways players interact with their game, as well as the metrics for each interaction. For example, taking cover is one of the main mechanics in <em>Uncharted 4</em>. And there are 2 types of cover: high and low. Each of them is bound to a specific range of numbers to provide a consistent visual language. That means every object intended for cover must follow these metrics.</p>\n\n<p>You can see the impact of system designers most prominently in games like <em>Dota 2</em> and <em>League of Legends</em><em>. </em>These games have to regularly roll out gameplay patches for competitive seasons and to maintain the interest in the playerbase.</p>\n\n<h2>Combat design</h2>\n\n<p>Combat design is a branch of system design, which covers specific areas such as battle systems, enemy design, and encounter design. We'll go through these three briefly.</p>\n\n<p>First, we have the battle system, which is the framework for the combat. First, the designer will decide whether their game has real-time or turn-based combat. Then, they'll decide on the player's toolkit: what action the player avatar can perform and how it is performed. For example, Hornet in <em>Hollow Knight Silksong</em> can dash to quickly get out of harm's way; this action takes 35 frames in total to complete.</p>\n\n<p>Enemy designers come up with adversaries for the player. Ideally, each enemy in the game fulfills a distinct role in their quest to oppose the player.</p>\n\n<p>Encounter designers build the combat scenarios for the game. They decide how many enemies and which enemies are included in each combat encounter. Their goal is to provide an engaging and fair combat experience to players.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/c66ab8ab16822a4b978a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/c66ab8ab16822a4b978a.webp\" width=\"802\" height=\"685\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/ab8fab3102bb5e42b941.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/ab8fab3102bb5e42b941.webp\" width=\"1041\" height=\"638\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/9c49db1dc433735a9ba9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/9c49db1dc433735a9ba9.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"612\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/b5a1ed389a3ed300f292.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/b5a1ed389a3ed300f292.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"610\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Level design</h2>\n\n<p>Level designers map out the playgrounds for their games. They use grayboxes to set up the objective and events of the level. You can liken level designers to urban planners. They both build spaces that people can explore at their own pace, but add subtle hints in the environment to nudge their explorers to the intended path.</p>\n\n<p>For an in-depth look into level creation, visit our Level Art Fundamentals!</p>\n\n<h2>For further research</h2>\n\n<p>As an introduction to the concept, we don't want to overwhelm you with too much information, but we don't want to leave you hanging either. So, here are some other design roles you can look into.</p>\n\n<p>The Lead Designer establishes a unified vision for the game design. As the title implies, this person helms the whole design process and approves design ideas submitted by others. He's like an Art Director, but for designers.</p>\n\n<p>Technical designers investigate the programming side of game development. They explore the existing technologies and maybe invent new ones to power their games.</p>\n\n<p>Finally, monetization designers focus on how their games generate profits to propose suitable business models.</p>\n\n<p>Thank you for reading, and happy gaming!</p>";
